System Design [Partioning]

Data is big for one server. Break it to mutiple nodes.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
										TYPE OF PARTITIONING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Key Range Based:
	Lets say we have user with name A-Z in system.. We can partition them as A-C, D-F .... X-Z
	
	Pros: Similar keys end up in same partition. Eg: Range Query on Name will work great here.
	Cons: Hotstop can occur in partition.

2. Key Hash Based:

	Pros: Relatively Even distribution, less hot spots
	Cons: Range Queries are not efficient.
	Note: We can use secondary index to optimize range queries in Hash based sharding.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
										SECONDARY INDEX
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Secondary indexes allow querying data using attributes other than the primary partition key.

1. Local Secondary Index: LSI is stored within the same partition as the primary data. Each primary partition maintains its own secondary index(say based on key B).

Pros: For Range Queries, we still have to read all partitions(based on A) but atleast we have secondary index (based on B) on each of them.
	  Strongly Consistent with writes to base data as changes happen in same partition where secondary index resides.
Cons: Range Query based on secondary index still need to read to all partitions.
	  Tail Latency Amplification : Range Query is limited to speed of slowest parition.
	  Write Amplification: For each write, we also have to update index.

2. Global Secondary Index: GSI is partitioned independently of the primary data. All entries for the same indexed key (say B was GSI key) are stored in the same GSI partition, regardless of where the base data (partitioned by A) resides.
Note: To avoid blocking clinets, update to GSI are asynchronous whenerver write to Base data is made.

Pros: Range Queries (based on GSI) can be served with one GSI Partition.
Cons: GSIs require their own partitioning strategy. (They may use same nodes used by Base tables though)
	  Eventual Consistent: Clients may temporarily see stale secondary index results due to aysnc GSI updates on base table writes.

